===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|eih}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*aiks}}; akin to Low German {{m|nds|Eek}}, Dutch {{m|nl|eik}}, West Frisian {{m|fy|iik}}, English {{m|en|oak}}, Danish {{m|da|eg}}, Swedish {{m|sv|ek}}. Ultimately derived from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*eiǵ-}}.

''Further Indo-European cognates:'' Latin {{m|la|aesculus||Durmast oak}}, Latvian {{m|lv|ozols}}, Lithuanian {{m|lt|ąžuolas}}, Albanian {{m|sq|enjë||juniper, yew}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|[ˈaɪ̯çə]|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-Eiche.ogg|audio|lang=de}}
* {{rhymes|aɪ̯çə|lang=de}}

===Noun===
{{de-noun|f||Eichen}}

# {{label|de|countable}} An [[oak tree]], an [[oak]], ''[[Quercus]]''; the common tree
#: '''''Eichen''' können über tausend Jahre alt werden.'' — '''Oak trees''' can reach an age of more than one thousand years.
#: ''Kork'''eiche''''' — cork '''oak'''
# {{label|de|uncountable}} [[oak]]; the wood of the tree

